---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { getState, href } from '../../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbNode, pageGraph, webPageNode } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'Arizona HOA rules for an RV or boat | RV Storage Prices';
const description =
  'No Arizona statute found that sets an RV or boat parking rule for a planned community. A.R.S. 33-1809 and 33-1818 cover other vehicles and public roads. Not legal advice.';
const path = '/rules/arizona/';
const checked = '2026-11-19';
const arizona = getState('arizona');
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { label: 'HOA rules', path: '/rules/' },
  { label: 'Arizona', path },
];
const schema = pageGraph([
  webPageNode({ title, description, path, dateModified: checked }),
  breadcrumbNode(crumbs),
]);
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>Arizona HOA rules for an RV or boat</h1>
    <p>
      This page is not legal advice. On <time datetime={checked}>November 19, 2026</time>, no Arizona
      statute was found that names a recreational vehicle, motor home, or boat as something a planned
      community must allow, or must forbid, on a lot or a driveway.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two nearby sections were read on the Arizona Legislature site. Neither one is an RV or boat rule.
      A third section uses the words “recreational vehicle” for a rented space in a park, not for a lot
      in a planned community.
    </p>
    <h2>Employment vehicles, not rigs</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.azleg.gov/ars/33/01809.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer">A.R.S. § 33-1809</a>
      is titled “Parking; public service and public safety emergency vehicles; definition.” Subsection A
      says:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Notwithstanding any provision in the community documents, an association shall not prohibit a
        resident from parking a motor vehicle on a street or driveway in the planned community if the
        vehicle is required to be available at designated periods at the person's residence as a
        condition of the person's employment and either of the following applies:
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The two conditions that follow are a public-service vehicle of 20,000 pounds gross vehicle weight
      rating or less, and a public-safety vehicle of 10,000 pounds or less. Each must bear an official
      emblem or other visible designation. The section does not name a recreational vehicle or a boat.
      This page does not treat that sentence as a rule for an RV.
    </p>
    <h2>Public roadways</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.azleg.gov/ars/33/01818.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer">A.R.S. § 33-1818</a>
      is titled “Community authority over public roadways; vote of the membership; applicability.”
      Subsection A says that for a planned community whose declaration is recorded after December 31,
      2014, after the period of declarant control, and notwithstanding any provision in the community
      documents, an association “has no authority over and shall not regulate any roadway for which the
      ownership has been dedicated to or is otherwise held by a governmental entity.” Subsection C says
      the section “does not apply to any one-way streets, without regard to ownership, or to any
      privately owned roadways.” The section does not name a recreational vehicle or a boat. Older
      declarations have a membership-vote path in subsection B. This page does not decide how that vote
      came out in any community.
    </p>
    <h2>An RV park rental is a different statute</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.azleg.gov/ars/33/02101.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer">A.R.S. § 33-2101</a>
      says its chapter applies to a recreational vehicle space rented in a recreational vehicle park or
      mobile home park by the same tenant for more than 180 consecutive days. That is landlord-tenant
      law for a park space. It is not a rule for parking on a planned-community lot.
    </p>
    <h2>What the silence does not decide</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No statute found is not a statement that an association may not adopt a parking rule. The
        declaration, the bylaws, and the city or county code still have to be read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.azleg.gov/ars/33/01802.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer">A.R.S. § 33-1802</a>
        defines “planned community” for that chapter and excludes a condominium governed by chapter 9
        and a timeshare governed by chapter 20. This page does not read a condominium document.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Off-site storage is a different question</h2>
    <p>
      A facility listing in this directory is a storage lot, not a statement that an association allows
      the vehicle at the house. {arizona.cities.length} Arizona city pages list those lots.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        arizona.cities.map((city) => (
          <li>
            <a href={href(`/cities/${city.id}/`)}>
              {city.city}, {city.state_code}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
